AB  - With the continual improvement of remote sensing spatial resolution, fine-grained interpretation of internal elements in open-pit mining areas has become increasingly feasible and important. However, existing mining-related studies have largely overlooked such element-level interpretation, partly because internal mining components in very-high-resolution imagery often exhibit irregular shapes, fragmented spatial distributions, ambiguous boundaries, and high visual similarity, and partly because dedicated datasets with pixel-level annotations remain limited. To address these issues, this study constructs a very-high-resolution dataset for fine-grained semantic segmentation of open-pit mining areas in Yunnan Province, China, named Yunnan Open-pit Mining Areas Dataset (YOMAD), which contains 2197 image patches with pixel-level annotations for five categories. Based on YOMAD, we propose SparX-Mamba-based feature alignment and edge-guided segmentation network (SFAE-Net), a dedicated segmentation framework composed of a SparX-Mamba encoder, a feature alignment attention (FAA)-enhanced UPer decoder, and an edge-guided auxiliary branch. The SparX-Mamba encoder enhances hierarchical contextual representation, enabling the model to distinguish mining elements with similar local appearances but different spatial and functional contexts. The FAA-enhanced UPer decoder alleviates cross-scale spatial misalignment during top-down feature fusion, thereby improving the recognition of small, fragmented, and irregularly distributed objects. The edge-guided auxiliary branch further strengthens boundary-aware feature learning and helps delineate ambiguous transitions between adjacent mining elements. Experiments on YOMAD show that SFAE-Net achieves 70.45% mean Intersection over Union (mIoU) and 77.476% mean accuracy (mAcc), outperforming representative semantic segmentation models and validating the effectiveness of the proposed components. These results demonstrate that the proposed dataset and model can provide effective support for fine-grained, management-oriented remote sensing interpretation of open-pit mining areas.
