AB  - The detection of open-pit mines from high-resolution remote sensing imagery holds significant ecological and economic importance. Previous methods treated open-pit mines as simple land cover types for extraction, often leading to confusion with bare land due to similarities in texture and color. This letter proposes a height knowledge-embedded network for open-pit mine extraction, namely, MHNet, leveraging elevation information to enhance mine detection. The framework consists of two key components: mine height simulation and height knowledge embedding. To obtain elevation information, a height simulator is introduced to model the elevation variations of mines. Through joint learning, the framework simultaneously supervises the localization of open-pit mines and the learning of elevation variations, enabling the extraction of more robust mine features. The proposed method automatically simulates pseudolabels for elevation estimation using the mining patterns of open-pit mines, making the proposed framework compatible with arbitrary semantic segmentation models and eliminating the need for additional sample collection costs. The proposed method demonstrates superior performance on a self-constructed high-resolution remote sensing open-pit mine extraction dataset.
