PY  - 2026
VL  - 64
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/TGRS.2026.3708813
SN  - 1558-0644
AB  - Disturbance detection and restoration monitoring are essential in mining regions for understanding landscape dynamics and supporting restoration decision-making. However, traditional remote sensing approaches often suffer from sparse temporal observations and phenological noise, which limit their capacity to capture subtle or short-term ecological dynamics. To overcome these issues, we develop an enhanced intensive time series LandTrendr (ITSLT) framework based on dense Landsat time series derived from all available surface reflectance imagery from 2000 to 2023, and integrate key temporal breakpoints for improved disturbance extraction. In addition, a dynamic time warping (DTW)-based trajectory similarity analysis is introduced to classify disturbance–recovery patterns and quantify deviation from reference baseline sequences. Applied to the Yuzhong mining area in central Henan, China, the proposed method improves the temporal sensitivity and robustness of disturbance characterization. Results show pronounced spatial heterogeneity in disturbance magnitude and recovery trajectories. Human-induced disturbances account for 52.91% of all events while mining-related impacts contribute 2.61% and 5.98% of the total area, highlighting the dominant role of anthropogenic activities. Although regional ecosystem conditions exhibit an overall recovery trend, they have not yet returned to the levels observed in 2000. Disturbances have progressively expanded from core mining zones toward adjacent construction and transportation corridors, reflecting a shift from localized to more spatially connected anthropogenic impacts. Our combined ITSLT with DTW framework provides an effective tool for long-term disturbance detection and ecological recovery assessment in mining landscapes, and supports application in ecological restoration monitoring and green mine planning.
