AB  - Aiming at the critical problem of limited labeled samples in the joint classification of synthetic aperture radar (SAR) image and hyperspectral image (HSI), this paper proposes a dual-domain collaborative semi-supervised network (DCSNet). The DCSNet first employs two separate dual-domain feature mining (DFM) modules to perform feature extraction for SAR and HSI data, respectively. Each DFM module comprises two branches: one for learning local neighborhood information in the spatial domain, and the other for capturing global semantic information in the frequency domain. On this basis, a hierarchical consistency interaction (HCI) mechanism is further designed. It performs mutual supervision on the cross-modal outputs of the global semantic features, and mines similar sample pairs to implement cross-sample mutual supervision within the same modal branch, so as to fully exploit the potential supervision information in unlabeled samples. Experimental results on public dataset demonstrate that, with limited labeled samples, the proposed algorithm achieves a significant improvement in classification accuracy compared with related methods, enabling high-precision semi-supervised classification for SAR-HSI fusion.
