AB  - Ensuring public safety and long-term structural sustainability requires efficient and accurate monitoring of infrastructure problems. The first signs of structural deterioration are cracks in the ground or in the building itself. Traditional visual inspection techniques, however, are still flawed in that they are subjective, labor-intensive, and might endanger field personnel. These constraints highlight the need of a quantifiable, objective, and automated method for fracture identification, especially in high-resolution photogrammetric images acquired from drone or ground-based aerial surveys. By including the ResNet-101 backbone and using Conditional Random Fields (CRF) as a post-processing step to modify segmentation boundaries, this work suggests improving the DeepLabV3+ semantic segmentation model. With ResNet-101, we can extract more hierarchical features, and with CRF, we can fix local inconsistencies and smooth out crack borders on the probability map of our predictions. Images of ground cracks from both lab experiments and field observations are part of the Landslide Crack Dataset, which was used to create a binary ground-truth mask. All of the data in the dataset was personally annotated. In order to enhance pixel segmentation accuracy, boundary sharpness, and model robustness in detecting thin cracks in complex and heterogeneous photogrammetric environments, this research primarily focuses on integrating the DeepLabV3+ framework with a deeper backbone and CRFbased refinement. The suggested model outperforms the DeepLabV3+ baseline model experimentally, with scores of 0.845 for Dice Score, 0.158 for Dice Loss, and an average Intersection over Union (IoU) value of 0.272. These results show that the suggested method can back up monitoring systems for mining environments and large-scale infrastructure with a trustworthy and unbiased solution.
