AB  - Hyperspectral (HS) remote sensing offers superior spectral resolution for mineral identification compared to multispectral (MS) data. However, HS sensors present a tradeoff between spatial resolution and coverage; high-resolution sensors like AVIRIS and HISUI are typically limited to narrow swaths, while some new sensors designed for wide-swath coverage, such as EMIT, consequently have a coarser spatial resolution. In contrast, MS imagery (e.g., ASTER) provides more comprehensive regional coverage. In many operational scenarios, MS images cover an entire region of interest, while HS data is available for only a sub-region. This discrepancy motivates the development of reliable MS-based classifiers that can be trained or validated using partial HS data. This study proposes a domain adaptation methodology to enhance the generalization of MS-based mineral classification across different geographical areas. The proposed method, distinct in its data-driven approach, derives feature transformation rules to bridge the spectral disparities between a source domain (training site) and a target domain (evaluation site). The process begins by identifying corresponding pixel clusters between sites based on spectral similarity (cosine similarity) for each mineral class. From these corresponding pixels, a multitude of candidate features—including band reflectance, adjacent band differences, and various spectral indices-are generated. Single regression models are then trained for each feature to map the target domain's spectral values to the source domain's values. A selection of the most stable and highly correlated transformation models, evaluated by their coefficient of determination and residual standard deviation, is used to generate a new set of transformed features for both domains. These transformed features are concatenated with the original MS spectral data to form an augmented feature set. This augmented set enables a Random Forest classifier to learn from both the original spectral information and the domain-invariant transformed information. The framework was validated through a comprehensive cross-transfer experiment involving three arid regions in the southwestern United States: Cuprite, Gold Field, and Silver Peak. ASTER data was used as an MS data for classification, with HS data from AVIRIS, EMIT, and HISUI serving as ground-truth references. The results demonstrate that the proposed method significantly improves the portability and robustness of MS-based classifiers across geographically and spectrally diverse regions, supporting the operational use of widely available MS archives for large-scale mineral mapping, especially when HS data for calibration and validation is limited.
