AB  - Histopathological diagnosis remains the gold standard for confirming gastric tubular adenocarcinoma. As a central task in computer-aided diagnosis, pathological image segmentation plays a crucial role in enhancing both diagnostic efficiency and accuracy. Microscopic hyperspectral images (MHSIs) have demonstrated substantial potential by providing distinctive spectral signatures, while conventional RGB images offer richer spatial textures and fine-grained structural details. The effective fusion of these complementary modalities can significantly boost segmentation performance. However, current deep learningbased multimodal segmentation methods often fail to fully exploit the unique attributes of each modality. These approaches mostly rely on complex fusion architectures with limited interpretability, ultimately leading to suboptimal outcomes. To address these challenges, we propose a novel handcrafted feature-guided multimodal fusion segmentation framework for gastric pathology segmentation. Our approach leverages statistically meaningful handcrafted spatial features extracted from RGB images to guide the deep network in learning more modality-sensitive and discriminative representations. Considering the structural discrepancies between 2D RGB and 3D MHSI, we propose three feature fusion strategies that enhance cross-modal complementarity and support efficient and robust segmentation. Experiments on a multimodal gastric pathology dataset demonstrate that our method effectively segments tumor, stroma, and space regions. Compared to TransUNet, it achieves a $\mathbf{1 0. 1 \%}$ increase in IoU, and a 7.4 % reduction in Hausdorff Distance. Ablation studies further confirm that handcrafted feature guidance consistently enhances segmentation performance across all three fusion strategies.
KW  - Gastric tubular adenocarcinoma
KW  - pathology segmentation
KW  - hyperspectral and RGB images
KW  - multimodal feature fusion
KW  - handcrafted feature guidance
