AB  - This study employs geospatial technologies, remote sensing data, and data mining techniques to evaluate land use and land cover (LULC) changes and urban expansion in Hanoi, the capital of Vietnam, during 2018, 2020 and 2024. An unsupervised machine learning framework was implemented to analyze urban growth patterns using spectral bands and indices derived from Sentinel-2 imagery. Data were acquired from Google Earth Engine (GEE), preprocessed, and standardized using the StandardScaler method. The K-Means clustering algorithm was applied to group surface cover classes with similar spectral characteristics, while Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was used for dimensionality reduction and visualization. The results demonstrate that spectral indices such as NDVI, NDBI and MNDWI effectively distinguish urban areas, vegetation, water bodies, and bare land, enabling a quantitative assessment of urban expansion over time. These finding provide scientific basis for assessing urban dynamics and support data-driven urban planning and sustainable development strategies.
