PY  - 2026
VL  - 19
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/JSTARS.2026.3667805
SN  - 2151-1535
AB  - The formation of traditional Chinese village landscapes profoundly reflects the long-term interaction between humans and the land, as well as ecological wisdom. Systematically exploring their multidimensional wisdom and elucidating natural adaptive mechanisms serves as an effective pathway for cultural preservation. This study examines 415 traditional Chinese villages in the Qiandongnan region to explore landscape classification systems, morphological patterns, and their natural adaptive mechanisms. Results indicate: 1) Topography and landforms are the dominant factors shaping landscape differentiation, demonstrating the rigid constraints of natural foundations on spatial patterns. 2) Mountain plateau-type villages adhere to the site selection principle of “facing the sun while sheltering from the shade, with mountains behind and water in front,” forming a vertical zonation structure of “residential core-terrace farming-forest protection.” Tree-like street layouts and single-nucleus settlement patterns adapt to steep terrain; 3) River valley terrace villages follow the principle of “settling near water and building close to fields,” forming a parallel zonal system of “waterfront dwellings-terrace farming-bank protection forests.” Fishbone-patterned streets and multinucleated, bead-like building clusters adapt to the constraints of elongated terrain; 4) Peak-and-depression karst villages primarily follow the principle of “building settlements around peaks and choosing depressions for habitation,” forming a centripetal layered structure of “depression core - ring-shaped cultivation - peak-and-depression protection.” They adapt to extreme karst topography through grid-like streets and multinucleated dispersed layouts. 5) Villages exhibit a construction logic of “following mountain contours, aligning with water flow, and leveraging terrain advantages,” revealing a natural adaptive mechanism of “reliance-conformity-utilization-integration.”
