TI  - Peripheral Oxygen Saturation Measurement: Could the Wrist be an Alternative Site for a Finger?
JO  - 2023 IEEE World Conference on Applied Intelligence and Computing (AIC)
PY  - 2023
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/AIC57670.2023.10263821
AB  - Wearable devices capable of monitoring oxygen saturation levels have become increasingly popular due to their convenience, portability, and potential for continuous monitoring. The variation in $\text{SpO}_{2}$ measurements at the finger and wrist depends on the penetration depth of the wavelengths, signal quality, and signal-to-noise ratios since these regions have different blood perfusion characteristics and vascular structures. There are limitations in using the Red and IR Wavelengths, which are used in conventional pulse oximeters, to be employed for the wrist region. The evaluation of the effect of different combinations of wavelengths at the finger and wrist demonstrates which region provides higher accuracy at multiple wavelengths. Since the fingertip-attached devices limit users' ability to move their hands freely, efforts have been taken to design and validate wrist-worn technologies to measure $\text{SpO}_{2}$. Method: A Multispectral-based wrist-worn wearable Photoplethysmographic (MSPPG) device employs an optical bio-sensing, integrated BIOFY sensor to acquire the Photoplethysmographic (PPG) signals using three LEDs (Green-526nm, Red-660 nm, and IR- $950\text{nm}$) and photo-detectors. The signals are acquired from the finger as well as from the wrist region of healthy volunteers, which are processed to obtain the $\text{SpO}_{2}$ measurements keeping a standard pulse oximeter as a gold standard reference. The $\text{SpO}2$ obtained using the MSPPG signal from the finger and wrist is compared with the standard pulse oximeter. The comparison is made between $\text{Standard}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2}$ and $\text{Finger}_{-}\text{MSPPG}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2}, \text{Standard}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2}$ and $\text{Wrist}_{-}\text{MSPPG}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2},\text{Finger}_{-}\text{MSPPG}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2}$, and $\text{Wrist}_{-}\text{MSPPG}_{-}\text{SpO}_{2}$. The statistical analysis is performed using the Student's paired t-test and Pearson correlation coefficient, which confirms that the wrist shows greater accuracy while using the Green and IR combination of wavelengths. Conclusion: Wearable PPG-based devices face a significant issue of motion artifacts which can be overcome by using MSPPG. The research advancements can potentially increase the accessibility of $\text{SpO}_{2}$ monitoring in resource-limited areas
