JO  - 2025 IEEE 4th International Conference of Safe Production and Informatization (IICSPI)
PY  - 2025
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/IICSPI66775.2025.11437595
AB  - Ship detection is a key support for maritime traffic safety and port scheduling, but complex sea conditions, ship scale differences and occlusion problems, coupled with the limitations of low infrared image resolution, high cost of remote sensing images and insufficient timeliness, make it difficult to detect objects and insufficient occlusion processing in shore-mounted visible light scenarios become the core bottlenecks. Based on YOLOv8 and combined with the idea of load test data mining, an improved YOLOv8 algorithm is proposed: First, a high-frequency enhanced residual block is designed to replace the middle-layer C2f module of the backbone network, and enhances the ability to capture high-frequency information via the “channel splitting-dual-branch extraction-fusion residual” architecture, which helps to mine detailed features such as ship edge and small target contour in load test data, and solves the problem of feature loss at multiple scales. The second is to introduce Occlusion-aware Attention-based Detection Head with occlusion perception attention at the detection head, and generate attention maps through multi-head deep separable convolution, fully connected layer and feature fusion, so as to strengthen the mining of target features in the occlusion area in the load test data and alleviate the positioning bias and feature loss. The overall average precision at 0.5 intersection over union (IoU) of the model reaches 0.842, which has excellent detection accuracy for ore ships, passenger ships, container ships and other types of ships, especially in the robustness of small target recognition and occlusion scenarios, which is better than the mainstream ship detection methods, which fully proves the effectiveness of the algorithm in the fusion application of load test data mining and ship detection. In summary, the improved framework proposed in this paper combined with data mining technology effectively improves the accuracy and overall robustness of ship detection in complex scenarios of shoreborn-borne visible light, and provides a better technical solution for actual port and waterway supervision.
