AB  - To address the problem of insufficient detection accuracy of traditional YOLO models due to the "dense small targets, large scale differences, and susceptibility to occlusion" in remote sensing images, this paper designs an innovative YOLO model integrating multi-scale features. First, a dynamic receptive field multi-scale feature extraction module is constructed, using three parallel branches with differentiated expansion rates and gating units to selectively capture features of targets at different scales. Second, an attention-driven adaptive feature fusion strategy is proposed, dynamically allocating cross-layer feature weights to enhance the utilization of details in small targets and semantic information in large targets. Finally, a small target weight factor and a hard sample mining coefficient are introduced on top of the CIoU loss to optimize the model's learning bias. Experiments are conducted using the NWPU VHR-10 and a self-made UAV remote sensing dataset as test sets, comparing YOLOv5s, YOLOv7-tiny, and YOLOv8s models. Results show that on the NWPU VHR-10 dataset, the proposed model achieves 92.3% mAP@0.5 (a 3.2% improvement over YOLOv8s) and 88.7% mAP-small (a 5.1% improvement); on the self-made dataset, mAP@0.5 reaches 90.5% (a 2.8% improvement), while maintaining an FPS of 28 frames/second (on par with YOLOv8s). This research demonstrates that the proposed model can effectively improve the accuracy of multi-scale target detection in remote sensing images, while also considering real-time performance, making it suitable for scenarios such as land monitoring and agricultural yield estimation.
