AB  - Soil heavy metals pose a severe threat to human health and ecosystem stability. However, localized spatial disturbances caused by environmental factors or mining activities can induce considerable heterogeneity in heavy metal distribution, thereby reducing the inversion accuracy for soil heavy metal levels. To address these issues, a novel Stacking partial least squares regression (Stacking-PLSR) ensemble learning method was proposed for the inversion of soil heavy metals (Cu, Zn, and Pb) levels. Initially, hyperspectral data were preprocessed using various spectral transformation techniques. The results showed that spectral transformations, particularly the successive application of second and first derivatives (SD-FD), significantly improved the sensitivity of soil reflectance spectra to heavy metals. The Stacking-PLSR model with SD-FD transformation achieved the best inversion accuracy compared with other models. This result demonstrated that the Stacking-PLSR model can robustly and accurately predict heavy metal levels using measured spectral data. Furthermore, spatial distribution maps of soil heavy metals were constructed based on the optimal model outputs. The mapping results showed that Cu contamination in agricultural soils was the most pronounced. This finding implied that the mining area was the primary source of heavy metal pollution in the study area. The study demonstrated that hyperspectral inversion holds potential for monitoring soil heavy metals. It can guide risk assessment and remediation of heavy metal contamination in farmland soils near mining areas.
