AB  - Climate change has increased the frequency and spread of wheat stripe rust, posing a serious threat to wheat yields and food security. Traditional manual field surveys are inefficient, highly subjective, and limited in coverage, making it difficult to meet the demands of large-scale, precise monitoring and forecasting. Remote sensing technology, with its advantages of non-destructive, rapid, and large-scale acquisition of wheat phenotypic and spectral information, has become a core tool for wheat disease monitoring. Although current remote sensing has made some progress, significant shortcomings remain in analyzing the early physiological mechanisms and spectral response characteristics of wheat stripe rust, developing highly sensitive early-stage monitoring methods, and achieving collaborative fusion and information mining of multi-source remote sensing data. These shortcomings lead to low monitoring accuracy and high rates of missed or false detections for early-stage stripe rust. To address this, this paper proposes a vegetation detection model based on the Continuous Wavelet-Guided Dual-Stream Fusion Network with Multi-Scale Attention (CW-DSF-MSA). By performing multi-scale feature enhancement via continuous wavelet transform, deep feature extraction and fusion using a dual-stream architecture, and key feature focusing through a multi-scale attention mechanism, the model achieves efficient fusion and precise extraction of multi-source remote sensing features. It effectively elucidates the synergistic diagnostic mechanism of the solar-induced chlorophyll fluorescence (SIF)-reflectancetemperature triplet, leverages the complementary advantages of multi-source data, significantly improves the sensitivity and accuracy of early-stage wheat stripe rust monitoring, and resolves the technical bottleneck of accurately identifying the disease at an early stage. This study provides new technical methods and theoretical support for early warning, precise prevention and control of wheat stripe rust, and food security.
