AB  - Coal mining suffers heavily from illegal mines in different paradigms of social, economical, and environmental welfare of a country. It is complex to separate legal and illegal mines using spectral characteristics exclusively. As coal dusts spreads along the surroundings of coal transportation, detection of roads, which are used for transporting raw coals from the other roads through spectral properties can be found instrumental for locating such illegal mines. In this paper, a novel technique has been proposed to detect such roads by analyzing their spectral properties without any labelled dataset. First, road extraction index (REI) is studied to detect probable roads. As coal mine index (CMI) preserves high mineral content regions, CMI values of these detected roads are examined. With varying threshold values of CMI the proposed technique can separate the roads inside a mining region, roads leaving a mining region, and roads not being used for transporting coals.
