AB  - The key to visible-infrared object detection lies in fully exploiting information from each modality to achieve effective complementarity. Currently, dual-stream fusion architectures incorporating transformers are challenged by the suppression of detailed features and insufficient mining of common features across modalities. This article proposes PND-Net, a framework that includes the pixel-wise attention (PWA), neighborhood interaction fusion (NIF), and dynamic feature mining (DFM) modules, which effectively enhance details in each modality and improve feature fusion from local to global, while collaboratively mining common and unique features of each modality. Specifically, PWA learns channel-spatial attention without pooling-based feature compression to preserve fine details. NIF couples a sliding-window mechanism with self-attention to model efficient local-to-global cross-modal interactions. DFM employs modality-dependent dynamic convolution and a differential mask to emphasize modality-specific responses. A bifocal loss further guides the network to capture both commonality and uniqueness across modalities. Extensive experiments on vehicle detection in aerial imagery, DroneVehicle, and a visible-infrared paired dataset for low-light Vision (LLVIP) show that PND-Net achieves superior performance over representative state-of-the-art multispectral fusion detectors.
