AB  - This work presents the development and preliminary validation of a low-cost, portable optical device capable of measuring transmittance, reflectance, and luminescence in both liquid and paper-based samples. The system emerges from a multidisciplinary collaboration between electronic engineers and analytical chemists, aiming to democratize access to advanced optical sensing technologies. The instrument integrates a multispectral sensor with an optical filtering system, enabling time-multiplexed detection of multiple analytes with high selectivity. Modular sample holders accommodate standard cuvettes and solid-phase substrates, enhancing the platform's versatility across diverse analytical formats. To demonstrate its analytical performance, the device was tested with nanostructured optical sensors based on gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) and quantum dots (QDs), showcasing its applicability in fluorescence and colorimetric assays. The result is a robust, user-friendly tool with strong potential for deployment in (bio)analytical diagnostics, environmental monitoring, and point-of-need testing.
