AB  - Areas where limestone is mined and cement is produced at large scale have undergone much environmental change over the past few years. This paper quantifies the extent of forest loss in the industrial landscapes on the basis of the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) based on the MODIS satellite imagery. Two time periods, pre-2010 (2000-2009) and post-2015 (2016- 2025) periods were contrasted to identify the long term vegetation trends. The NDVI thresholding was used to differentiate between forested and non-forested areas to be able to quantify the changes in the green cover. The interpretation shows that vegetation degradation significantly decreases and most of the major losses have been centered in the areas of cement production centers and mining areas. These results point to the mounting unease between the industrialization and the ecological equilibrium, and the necessity to plan the land-use in a more ecologically conscious way and to approach the long-term management of resources more ecologically responsible.
