AU  - C. O. Dumitru
AU  - N. Longepe
TI  - Hidden Scars in Green: Label-Efficient Mapping of Artisanal Mining in Forested Areas with Generative Foundation Models
JO  - 16th European Conference on Synthetic Aperture Radar (EUSAR)
PY  - 2026
PB  - VDE
AB  - Canopy cover, terrain shadowing, and persistent cloudiness limit optical monitoring of artisanal and small-scale gold min- ing (ASGM) in tropical forests. This work investigates a Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR)-only pathway by fine-tuning an earth observation (EO) foundation-model backbone on Sentinel-1 (S1) to delineate ASGM footprints with limited human annotations. Using the SmallMinesDS benchmark dataset from southwestern Ghana, we report segmentation metrics to- gether with compute indicators to enable cost-aware comparison. SAR-only fine-tuning yields reliable, fine-grained maps under canopy/cloud constraints (IoU/F1 0.5317/0.6942); with an S1-anchored Thinking-in-Modality (TiM) fusion strat- egy, a stepwise generative reasoning mechanism over input modalities, we further reach 0.5976/0.7470. Thus, this work demonstrates that TiM is order-sensitive: optimizing the modality sequence to Sentinel-2 (S2)→LULC→DEM→NDVI yields a +5.28%F1 improvement over the S1-only baseline, by chaining synthetically generated modalities with real S1 inputs. Where available, optical inputs remain the strongest single-modality signal, with early S1+S2 fusion provid- ing a small but consistent gain. Together, these results establish a practical, label-efficient route for all-weather ASGM mapping. 1
ER  -

